1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:14,000
Meet the Herding Scenario Lab. When AI trading agents share the same models they can herd,
and a small shock becomes a crash. Regulators need to know which surveillance tools would
catch it and which interventions would contain it, before it happens. This is where they
find out.

2
00:00:14,150 --> 00:00:26,630
Every run begins with the supervisor's real question: would a targeted throttle have
contained a flash crash with forty per cent of the market on one vendor's model, and which
surveillance tool deserves certification? The Lab turns that into a test.

3
00:00:26,780 --> 00:00:36,060
Next, the data: a file standing in for a venue's order flow, one row per participant per
minute. It is a synthetic sample; nothing here is confidential.

4
00:00:36,210 --> 00:00:43,940
Accepted, fingerprinted, classified, and used to calibrate the simulated markets, so the
tests reflect your market, not ours.

5
00:00:44,090 --> 00:00:50,790
And the first guardrail: a file containing an email address is refused outright. Personal
data never enters the sandbox.

6
00:00:50,940 --> 00:01:01,890
Plan. The Lab builds a battery of markets: strong herds, an evader, colluding agents, a
faulty vendor model, and the exact case the question asked about, all hashed, so what is
approved is what runs.

7
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:11,190
Now the human in the loop. Nothing runs without a named approver on the regulator's register
of authorised persons. This name is not on it: refused, and recorded.

8
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:19,210
R. Ahmed is on the register as an SMF24. He approves the battery, the approval is sealed
into the ledger, and the run begins.

9
00:01:19,360 --> 00:01:30,300
Watch the markets trade: ordinary traders, liquidity providers, agents on shared AI models,
and one cluster trying to ignite momentum. Eight surveillance tools watch every market as it
unfolds.

10
00:01:30,450 --> 00:01:45,310
On the tape, the shock lands and each tool's first alert shows. Then every intervention rule
is tried against every herd, and the assurance stages run: a guarantee on false alerts, a
red team attack on the certified tool, tail risk, time to crash, and a real data backtest.

11
00:01:45,460 --> 00:01:56,230
The market as a network. Squares are the agents that really caused the crash; amber rings
are what the tool flagged; red rings, the ones it missed. You see how good a tool is, not
just that an alarm went off.

12
00:01:56,380 --> 00:02:06,600
Before anyone can release a result, an independent critic recomputes every figure from the
raw data and replays the audit trail. Twenty eight checks passed; only now is release armed.

13
00:02:06,750 --> 00:02:14,070
Every step, approval and refusal is a signed, hash chained ledger entry. A third party can
replay the run and get the same answer.

14
00:02:14,220 --> 00:02:22,070
Seventeen security controls are checked live, from key management to the egress allowlist.
If one fails, release is blocked.

15
00:02:22,220 --> 00:02:36,890
And the finding that matters. By how well it mimics market statistics, the volatility
trigger comes first. By whether it catches the agents who cause the crash, it comes last.
That is the decision gap, and it is why the Lab certifies on the decision, with a guarantee
on false alerts.

16
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:40,420
Gate two. The same registered approver releases the briefing.

17
00:02:40,570 --> 00:02:52,640
The briefing answers the question asked, names the certified tool and its guarantee, scores
every rule on containment and false halts, and ships as an evidence pack with a transparency
record, ready for a board or a court.

18
00:02:52,790 --> 00:02:58,020
And a supervisor can simply ask the run. Herding Scenario Lab, from No Human Intelligence.

